.TH mbimagecorrect 1 "7 September 2026" "MB-System 5.0" "MB-System 5.0"
.SH NAME
\fBmbimagecorrect\fP \- Apply brightness and contrast corrections to a single image using OpenCV algorithms.

.SH VERSION
Version 5.0

.SH SYNOPSIS
mbimagecorrect [\fB\-\-verbose\fP \fB\-\-help\fP
\fB\-\-input=\fP\fIimagefile\fP \fB\-\-output=\fP\fIimagefile\fP
\fB\-\-histogram\-equalization\fP \fB\-\-gamma\-correction=\fP\fIgamma\fP
\fB\-\-clahe\-correction\fP \fB\-\-lightness\-correction=\fP\fIfactor\fP]

.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBMbimagecorrect\fP applies simple brightness and contrast corrections to a single
image using standard \fBOpenCV\fP algorithms. Four correction modes are available:
histogram equalization, gamma correction, contrast limited adaptive histogram
equalization (CLAHE), and a simple multiplicative lightness scaling. Only one
correction mode is applied per run; if no correction mode is specified on the
command line, CLAHE correction is used by default.
.P
Histogram equalization converts the image from BGR to YCrCb color space,
equalizes the histogram of the Y (luminance) channel only, and converts the
result back to BGR. Gamma correction builds a 256 entry lookup table using the
supplied gamma exponent and applies it independently to all three BGR channels
of every pixel. CLAHE correction converts the image to the Lab color space,
applies OpenCV's CLAHE algorithm (with a fixed clip limit of 4) to the L
(lightness) channel, and converts the result back to BGR. Lightness correction
converts the image to YCrCb color space and multiplies the Y channel of every
pixel by a constant factor, leaving the Cr and Cb (color) channels unchanged.
.P
The corrected image is written to the specified output file. \fBMbimagecorrect\fP
also opens two windows displaying the original and corrected images and waits
for a keypress before exiting; this program is not currently useful in a
non-interactive, headless batch processing context. Although the built in
usage message mentions supplying an imagelist as input, the current
implementation only reads a single image file named by \fB\-\-input\fP; it does not
read or iterate over an MB-System imagelist.
.P
Note that OpenCV enforces default maximum image dimensions when reading
images (2^20 pixels per side, 2^30 pixels total). To work with larger images,
set the environment variable \fBOPENCV_IO_MAX_IMAGE_PIXELS\fP before running the
program.

